    "created_at": "2022-08-31T18:01:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-08-31T18:01:31Z",
    "body": "The spec text is\r\n\r\n> The element's [src](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#attr-img-src), [srcset](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#attr-img-srcset), [width](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content-other.html#attr-dim-width), or [sizes](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#attr-img-sizes) attributes are set, changed, or removed.\r\n\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/images.html#relevant-mutations\r\n\r\nI think this predated DOM's [attribute change steps](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-element-attributes-change-ext) algorithm, so a bit unclear if setting to the same value counts or not. When I wrote the test I clearly had the interpretation that it should not count as a relevant mutation.\r\n\r\nI don't have a strong opinion about whether it *should* count. I'm only aware of web compat requirement for setting the `src` attribute to the same value, which is handled separately in the spec. But it seems a bit safer to align the spec and tests with WebKit and Chromium at this point. It's also more consistent to use DOM's attribute change steps.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
